WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.880
The NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0, often called CSF 2.0,

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.780
helps organizations manage and reduce cybersecurity risk.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.560
It gives teams a shared language for discussing cybersecurity outcomes, so executives,

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:20.540
managers, engineers, security teams, auditors, suppliers, and partners can talk about risk

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.920
without forcing every conversation into tool names or control numbers.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:30.340
That shared language is useful because cybersecurity decisions often involve tradeoffs

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:31.680
between mission,

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:36.380
technology, cost, time, resilience, and risk tolerance.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.720
CSF 2.0 is flexible by design.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:44.500
It is intended for organizations of different sizes, sectors, technology environments,

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:46.480
and maturity levels.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:51.260
Its main components are the Core, Organizational Profiles, and Tiers.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:56.120
A startup, hospital, manufacturer, school district, cloud team,

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:59.380
or government program may all use the framework differently.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.000
The framework does not say that every organization must implement the same controls in the

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.260
same order.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:10.920
Instead, it describes high-level outcomes that an organization can tailor to its context.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:16.180
It is also important to understand what CSF 2.0 is not.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.560
It is not a certification by itself, and it does not guarantee security.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:25.020
It is not a rigid checklist that replaces engineering judgment, legal review,

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.800
control design, or operational ownership.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:32.260
It helps organizations understand current risk, set target outcomes,

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:37.200
prioritize improvement, communicate decisions, and track progress over time.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:42.300
Used well, CSF 2.0 becomes a practical risk management tool.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:45.720
It helps an owner explain why an improvement plan matters,

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:50.580
why one gap is more urgent than another, what evidence supports the current assessment,

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.680
and how cybersecurity supports business objectives.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.140
The value is not in claiming alignment.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:00.280
The value is in clearer decisions, better prioritization,

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:02.740
and honest continuous improvement.
